# Trivy ignore list.
#
# Format: one CVE ID per line. Comments and blank lines are skipped.
# Trivy applies this file automatically when run from the repo root,
# so it covers both the publish.yml gate and local scans.
#
# Each entry MUST come with a comment block above it explaining:
#   - what the CVE is
#   - where it lives in the image
#   - why we accept it (instead of fixing)
#   - what would let us drop the ignore
#
# Audit posture: this file is intentionally short. If it grows past
# ~10 entries we've drifted from "accept upstream-only risk" to
# "ignore real findings" — at which point bump the toolkit's posture
# rather than the ignore list.

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# npm bundled libraries shipped with Node ≥ 20 (Nodesource package).
#
# These are libs (minimatch, picomatch, tar, cross-spawn) that npm
# itself bundles for its dependency resolver. The CVEs are real but:
#   - All exposed surface is in npm's archive / glob handling
#   - Workflows that use npm pull from trusted registries; the
#     vulnerable code paths require processing untrusted archives
#   - Patching them needs Node/Nodesource to ship a new package; not
#     fixable in this Dockerfile without breaking npm's self-update
#     loop ("Cannot find module 'promise-retry'" on `npm install -g
#     npm@latest` under Node 22 is a known regression)
#
# Drop these once Nodesource ships a Node 22.x release with newer
# bundled libraries (or Node 24 LTS, when it lands and we bump
# NODE_MAJOR).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CVE-2024-21538
